1
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:12,240
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of the, like, 32 parts of the body, you can't see your brain, your lungs, testes, etc.

2
00:00:13,401 --> 00:00:25,093
[SPEAKER_01]: So that, when you think about it, when you want to contemplate your brain, your lungs, then,

3
00:00:27,695 --> 00:00:29,476
[SPEAKER_01]: That idea is there.

4
00:00:29,816 --> 00:00:30,717
[SPEAKER_01]: That thought is there.

5
00:00:31,257 --> 00:00:31,757
[SPEAKER_01]: But is that...

6
00:00:32,138 --> 00:00:33,618
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now I'm attending that thought.

7
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:38,721
[SPEAKER_01]: And then I'm picking up the sign of those things.

8
00:00:38,982 --> 00:00:39,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

9
00:00:39,462 --> 00:00:40,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's... there's a difference there.

10
00:00:40,843 --> 00:00:42,384
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly...

11
00:00:42,524 --> 00:00:48,067
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way people would... people would approach this, the only way they would know how, is through attending.

12
00:00:49,208 --> 00:00:54,911
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, they would be attending that thought as if that's really attending to your brain, which you can't.

13
00:00:56,127 --> 00:01:06,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only way to see these body parts correctly, in the relevant contemplation manner, is indirectly, without attending them directly as objects of your attention.

14
00:01:06,713 --> 00:01:11,336
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to discern them as that because of which you are here living, attending to things.

15
00:01:13,747 --> 00:01:19,249
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the difference where people do... Well, that's the difference between doing the body contemplation rightly and wrongly.

16
00:01:19,269 --> 00:01:23,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Wrongly is basically just repeating these parts and visualizing them and thinking that that's somehow...

17
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:36,813
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it might provide you initial kind of... reduction of sensual craving, because you never think about those things, but soon... very soon you'll get used to it and it will become meaningless.

18
00:01:37,413 --> 00:01:39,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can't... you can't see those things directly.

19
00:01:39,254 --> 00:01:42,995
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why in Paticca Upādāna, the saḷāyātana, the sixth sense base,

20
00:01:43,993 --> 00:01:45,314
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the ultimate indirect.

21
00:01:45,574 --> 00:01:46,754
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the ultimate peripheral.

22
00:01:46,814 --> 00:01:51,216
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the ultimate that because of which there is the world for you.

23
00:01:51,276 --> 00:01:52,317
[SPEAKER_00]: You are perceiver.

24
00:01:52,337 --> 00:01:53,637
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the conceiver of it.

25
00:01:54,358 --> 00:02:03,462
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why when when the when the Buddha describes these the the six the six sense base, it's described in that almost external medical manner.

26
00:02:03,942 --> 00:02:05,923
[SPEAKER_00]: The fleshy eye, the nose and so on.

27
00:02:06,663 --> 00:02:09,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's that's the the closest you can see it.

28
00:02:10,025 --> 00:02:10,465
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

29
00:02:13,110 --> 00:02:14,631
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't see directly.

30
00:02:14,651 --> 00:02:16,213
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot attend to your eye.

31
00:02:16,673 --> 00:02:18,115
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only attend to the object.

32
00:02:20,036 --> 00:02:22,359
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not the eye because of which you see things.

33
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:28,905
[SPEAKER_00]: But you misconceiving it, that that's the eye because of which you see things, you start assuming that you can attend.

34
00:02:29,565 --> 00:02:31,828
[SPEAKER_00]: That because of which your attention is there.

35
00:02:33,029 --> 00:02:38,554
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to stop trying to attend the eye, the nose,

36
00:02:39,050 --> 00:02:45,836
[SPEAKER_00]: But start discerning it as a basis, as a physical basis for the world to exist for you.

37
00:02:47,757 --> 00:02:48,198
[SPEAKER_01]: But in...

38
00:02:49,519 --> 00:02:57,265
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the same sense, like, you can be attending at the cup, attending at the table, attending at the image of your brain.

39
00:02:59,828 --> 00:03:04,912
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're just regarding these things as overly objectified objects of your attention, so to speak.

40
00:03:06,877 --> 00:03:09,058
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, they have something in common.

41
00:03:09,278 --> 00:03:10,238
[SPEAKER_00]: They are all material.

42
00:03:10,958 --> 00:03:16,019
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't want to regard your brain just as you regard a cup, because cup is insignificant to you.

43
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:28,222
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to discern that that objective brain, that you are thinking about, is the reason for your subjectivity, because of which you are able to attend to the idea of the brain in the first place.

44
00:03:28,943 --> 00:03:30,083
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you just said there,

45
00:03:31,093 --> 00:03:34,214
[SPEAKER_01]: I must keep saying that to myself and again say that to myself.

46
00:03:34,234 --> 00:03:42,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you might have to initially to remind yourself to kind of recreate the right order of things that you can dwell on it.

47
00:03:44,498 --> 00:03:45,719
[SPEAKER_00]: But after a while, you don't have to.

48
00:03:45,959 --> 00:03:48,880
[SPEAKER_00]: After a while, it'll be enough just to... because you will know how to look indirectly.

49
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:51,281
[SPEAKER_00]: You will know how to see things without having to look at them.

50
00:03:52,321 --> 00:03:53,141
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's another thing.

51
00:03:53,442 --> 00:03:56,723
[SPEAKER_00]: I think people might misunderstand in these...

52
00:03:59,030 --> 00:04:09,757
[SPEAKER_00]: The the talks on YouTube and stuff, when we mention, you know, the simultaneous presence, people might end up thinking it's like two things at the same time momentarily present in front of me.

53
00:04:13,019 --> 00:04:20,805
[SPEAKER_00]: But when I say simultaneous presence, the way the suttas talk about it, it's basically two things are present on two different levels.

54
00:04:21,830 --> 00:04:29,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means they're not there equally present at the same time for a second or two and you must catch them both at the same time.

55
00:04:29,081 --> 00:04:29,261
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

56
00:04:29,341 --> 00:04:30,703
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just trying to catch one thing.

57
00:04:30,783 --> 00:04:33,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you can divide it into a million of sub-parts.

58
00:04:34,088 --> 00:04:37,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Your attention is still looking at that foreground of an object.

59
00:04:38,846 --> 00:04:43,148
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when we talk about simultaneous presence, that's why I keep saying, it endures.

60
00:04:43,349 --> 00:04:45,069
[SPEAKER_00]: The peripheral endures.

61
00:04:45,690 --> 00:04:56,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, in this case, say, you have a... you have a... an image of your brain, that you don't overly attend, as a random object in the world, that you tell then to yourself, I see this body is not mine.

62
00:04:57,396 --> 00:04:57,556
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

63
00:04:57,636 --> 00:05:04,320
[SPEAKER_00]: You see that image of the brain, you stop misconceiving it, as the Buddha would say, and you start discerning it.

64
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:05,701
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the closest.

65
00:05:07,041 --> 00:05:18,391
[SPEAKER_00]: The closest you can get to that which actually your life depends upon, to that form, the closest you can get is through these images of the brain, lungs, heart, blood, and so on.

66
00:05:19,192 --> 00:05:25,998
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize all of them stand for that because of which you are conscious here, living this life.

67
00:05:26,970 --> 00:05:33,515
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't possibly enter that brain, as in access it through your perception or anything like that, because it precedes it.

68
00:05:34,175 --> 00:05:46,724
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can know that the image of the brain that you have, that... on that peripheral level, is that because of which you are able to, again, be here and now, present and attend to these various other things that arise.

69
00:05:48,386 --> 00:05:51,328
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, then, when you stop thinking about the brain,

70
00:05:52,087 --> 00:05:59,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Your knowledge, that that's the order of your experience, as in you are undermined by the matter, doesn't change.

71
00:05:59,418 --> 00:06:01,441
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I mean it's simultaneously present.

72
00:06:01,741 --> 00:06:02,883
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to keep thinking.

73
00:06:02,943 --> 00:06:05,547
[SPEAKER_00]: You can forget about it, rightly.

74
00:06:06,464 --> 00:06:19,275
[SPEAKER_00]: It will basically constitute rightful forgetfulness, which is when your forgetfulness does not induce a vijja anymore, but you don't have to keep thinking... it's impossible to keep thinking about everything all the time.

75
00:06:19,295 --> 00:06:23,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I think sometimes you have to do.

76
00:06:23,198 --> 00:06:26,261
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to... because you can't really think... okay, brain...

77
00:06:26,301 --> 00:06:27,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

78
00:06:27,101 --> 00:06:33,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you just keep then forcing it into the foreground, and then you fail to see what the peripheral there is.

79
00:06:34,087 --> 00:06:41,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you have now overly misconceived that brain, and you're making it into an object of your perception, which is impossible for it to be.

80
00:06:42,134 --> 00:06:43,074
[SPEAKER_00]: I cannot see itself.

81
00:06:44,295 --> 00:06:47,118
[SPEAKER_01]: But, because one wants to defend...

82
00:06:47,158 --> 00:06:48,118
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me just say it like this.

83
00:06:48,198 --> 00:06:48,899
[SPEAKER_00]: I wanted to add this.

84
00:06:49,259 --> 00:06:52,482
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, say, you think about the brain right and peripheral.

85
00:06:52,502 --> 00:06:57,866
[SPEAKER_00]: You discern the image of the brain, and you recognize there is the brain in here, because of which my experience still exists.

86
00:06:58,847 --> 00:07:00,568
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you stop thinking about it.

87
00:07:01,169 --> 00:07:02,570
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, three hours later,

88
00:07:03,482 --> 00:07:04,903
[SPEAKER_00]: You think about it again.

89
00:07:05,563 --> 00:07:10,245
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's important here to... to... to induce the attitude, if you have to.

90
00:07:10,645 --> 00:07:12,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Eventually, you will recognize it as such.

91
00:07:13,386 --> 00:07:21,669
[SPEAKER_00]: It's important when, three hours later, you think about the brain again, you recognize that it's the same brain that has been here this whole time.

92
00:07:23,010 --> 00:07:26,371
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that you forgot about it, did not make it not there.

93
00:07:28,001 --> 00:07:30,923
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, even, like, same, like, with moods or feelings.

94
00:07:31,363 --> 00:07:35,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, you have a present mood, a reason, it's, it's, it's sort of, I don't know, agreeable.

95
00:07:35,987 --> 00:07:42,071
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you know, you, you start attending to more particular things, doing, and then you, you don't go around thinking how agreeable your day is.

96
00:07:42,751 --> 00:07:48,075
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, later, four, six hours later, you sort of look back and you remember it again, that it's agreeable.

97
00:07:48,175 --> 00:07:49,216
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same mood.

98
00:07:49,816 --> 00:07:51,638
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I mean, you have to let it endure.

99
00:07:52,078 --> 00:07:54,540
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, that means that when you bring it back,

100
00:07:55,656 --> 00:08:00,858
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't make it yours, but you recognize it as it has been enduring on its own.

101
00:08:02,898 --> 00:08:05,859
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you start discerning the simultaneous principle I'm talking about.

102
00:08:05,879 --> 00:08:15,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Simultaneous in the sense that, yeah, the general mood, the general notion of the matter being there, for example, in the contemplation of the body, it's been there enduring on its own.

103
00:08:17,163 --> 00:08:18,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you thought about it or not.

104
00:08:19,504 --> 00:08:20,624
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you do remember,

105
00:08:21,565 --> 00:08:27,447
[SPEAKER_00]: You will eventually stop giving priority to, I remember this, and I'm attending this, and I'm practicing this.

106
00:08:27,467 --> 00:08:28,367
[SPEAKER_00]: You just find it.

107
00:08:30,608 --> 00:08:34,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's simultaneously present with everything else you do, when you don't attend to that.

108
00:08:35,309 --> 00:08:39,070
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the necessary basis, basically, for your action, for your choice, for your attention.

109
00:08:41,731 --> 00:08:46,692
[SPEAKER_01]: But when can you... because, obviously, you want to develop the domain, as you say.

110
00:08:47,292 --> 00:08:48,693
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to develop the domain.

111
00:08:55,687 --> 00:09:00,548
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one wants to then develop it.

112
00:09:02,249 --> 00:09:03,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, okay, I've got nothing to do now.

113
00:09:03,869 --> 00:09:04,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

114
00:09:04,449 --> 00:09:05,370
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm going to develop that.

115
00:09:05,930 --> 00:09:10,451
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, I think about it for a while and then I forget about it.

116
00:09:10,511 --> 00:09:14,732
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop it by learning how to discern that it's already there.

117
00:09:15,573 --> 00:09:15,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

118
00:09:15,993 --> 00:09:16,353
[SPEAKER_01]: But then,

119
00:09:18,259 --> 00:09:19,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you have to forget about it.

120
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:21,640
[SPEAKER_01]: After a minute?

121
00:09:22,580 --> 00:09:23,520
[SPEAKER_00]: After a minute, that's fine.

122
00:09:24,020 --> 00:09:31,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, when you remember about it, you want to abandon the attitude of, I must restart this practice, because I forgot about it.

123
00:09:33,943 --> 00:09:36,623
[SPEAKER_00]: But instead, you want to develop recognition.

124
00:09:37,084 --> 00:09:38,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, it's still there.

125
00:09:41,185 --> 00:09:42,805
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much the crucial difference.

126
00:09:45,377 --> 00:09:51,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, I'm talking about where... because many people say, be here now, be present, just feel, just this, it's just like that and whatnot.

127
00:09:52,599 --> 00:09:56,421
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it all revolves just come back to the present moment, come back to the present moment.

128
00:09:56,921 --> 00:10:03,943
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to recognize that when you come back to the present moment, you could only do so because it was there and during beforehand.

129
00:10:04,043 --> 00:10:04,764
[SPEAKER_00]: It was always there.

130
00:10:05,184 --> 00:10:08,425
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you realize you can't forget about it even if you want.

131
00:10:09,852 --> 00:10:17,859
[SPEAKER_00]: You are responsible for your own ignorance, because you are responsible for the attitude of thinking it's on you to hold it in front of you, and that's the only way of existing.

132
00:10:18,559 --> 00:10:24,044
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you forget about it after a minute, then you remember it after five minutes, and then you think, oh, crap, I forgot about it.

133
00:10:24,424 --> 00:10:24,745
[SPEAKER_00]: I must...

134
00:10:24,885 --> 00:10:25,946
[SPEAKER_00]: I must do it again now.

135
00:10:26,867 --> 00:10:29,249
[SPEAKER_00]: As if you are the one who does it from the scratch.

136
00:10:29,889 --> 00:10:30,089
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

137
00:10:31,230 --> 00:10:36,535
[SPEAKER_00]: So, each time that attitude arises, you include that attitude in that mindfulness as well.

138
00:10:39,653 --> 00:10:40,733
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not rooted in you.

139
00:10:40,913 --> 00:10:42,894
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in the enduring background.

140
00:10:43,134 --> 00:10:47,995
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in... and that's what the mind is, by the way, the ultimate background, the citta nimitta.

141
00:10:48,215 --> 00:10:53,337
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why he knows the mind as exalted or as shrank or as depressed or as elated.

142
00:10:53,917 --> 00:10:59,259
[SPEAKER_00]: These states that people, obviously, almost automatically appropriate, are the states of mind.

143
00:10:59,479 --> 00:11:00,659
[SPEAKER_00]: As such, it endures.

144
00:11:01,279 --> 00:11:03,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Five days later, you remember it again.

145
00:11:03,940 --> 00:11:07,021
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason you can remember it is because it's still there, enduring.

146
00:11:08,925 --> 00:11:09,765
[SPEAKER_00]: To some extent.

147
00:11:11,306 --> 00:11:12,627
[SPEAKER_00]: And what will that do?

148
00:11:12,647 --> 00:11:18,949
[SPEAKER_00]: This kind of practice of mindfulness can... you cannot just... well, you can't be threading water with it.

149
00:11:19,989 --> 00:11:28,513
[SPEAKER_00]: If you keep doing it, it is forcing you, basically, to shift the center of your experience from your sense of self,

150
00:11:29,559 --> 00:11:34,844
[SPEAKER_00]: On to the background of the things, and even the actual things that you're attending, because you realize that they are there because of the background.

151
00:11:35,404 --> 00:11:38,126
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you start squeezing your own sense of self out.

152
00:11:38,307 --> 00:11:39,928
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no room for it in the experience.

153
00:11:40,609 --> 00:11:48,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Because whether you look at the background, whether you're attending the foreground, you realize this... it can be done so only because these things endure on their own, beforehand.

154
00:11:49,336 --> 00:11:56,242
[SPEAKER_00]: And in terms of the peripheral, as I said, six days later you remember something, it's because for six days it's been enduring there.

155
00:12:01,428 --> 00:12:07,891
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you stop worrying about, I must not forget, I must not forget.

156
00:12:08,791 --> 00:12:12,333
[SPEAKER_00]: But instead, start focusing on, I must discern whenever I remember.

157
00:12:12,833 --> 00:12:15,294
[SPEAKER_00]: I must not misconceive whenever I remember.

158
00:12:15,754 --> 00:12:18,375
[SPEAKER_00]: And the most fundamental misconceiving is mine.

159
00:12:18,896 --> 00:12:24,158
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, I am here at the center, not this thing that I remember because of which I am in the first place.

160
00:12:27,198 --> 00:12:42,003
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever your mind... whatever you attend to at any given time, that must induce a remembering of the way things are.

161
00:12:42,023 --> 00:12:43,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you don't even have to say that.

162
00:12:43,244 --> 00:12:48,205
[SPEAKER_00]: You can just say whatever you attend to, just be aware of what you're doing.

163
00:12:50,506 --> 00:12:52,587
[SPEAKER_00]: So, include yourself in that picture.

164
00:12:53,276 --> 00:12:56,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And that... where that sense of self is, that's already where the background is.

165
00:12:57,181 --> 00:13:04,710
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, instead of... instead of sort of feeling that each time you remember, you must restart and then hold as long as you can...

166
00:13:06,645 --> 00:13:19,313
[SPEAKER_00]: You drop that whole attitude, or stop paying attention to it, but instead, whenever you remember, even if it's once a day, on a day that you're very distracted, you still don't try to, like, oh, now I remember, I must hold it.

167
00:13:19,333 --> 00:13:19,493
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

168
00:13:19,813 --> 00:13:25,797
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember it as already there, arisen beforehand, not mine, not for me, not myself, cannot be mine.

169
00:13:27,097 --> 00:13:32,481
[SPEAKER_00]: And that automatically affects everything else within that background, within that peripheral.

170
00:13:34,522 --> 00:13:36,143
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's gonna take time to see that.

171
00:13:36,717 --> 00:13:39,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Because now, the emphasis is on what I attend.

172
00:13:39,798 --> 00:13:40,578
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what matters.

173
00:13:41,118 --> 00:13:42,438
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you try to keep...

174
00:13:42,458 --> 00:13:44,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so I'm attending on to this, I'm attending on to this.

175
00:13:44,639 --> 00:13:45,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you forget.

176
00:13:45,239 --> 00:13:47,659
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you think, oh, now I have to do it again.

177
00:13:48,180 --> 00:13:48,680
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you don't.

178
00:13:51,640 --> 00:13:55,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the only reason you can attend to anything is because it's there, given as an option.

179
00:13:55,801 --> 00:13:59,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means it exists on the peripheral, where the possibilities exist.

180
00:14:01,903 --> 00:14:03,683
[SPEAKER_00]: So, practically speaking, you think about

