375
00:25:42,488 --> 00:25:54,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not self-s... it's not rooted in you anymore, it's rooted in a... in a... well, in a proper foundation, which is the foundation of the... of the uncontrollable matter, the five aggregates that cannot actually be owned, and so on.

376
00:25:59,574 --> 00:26:09,337
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it becomes developed, or the knowledge is developed sufficiently enough, does it then... Dispassion.

377
00:26:10,637 --> 00:26:11,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Dispassion is the sign.

378
00:26:12,538 --> 00:26:13,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Dispassion is the sign.

379
00:26:13,638 --> 00:26:14,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Dispassion is the result.

380
00:26:15,619 --> 00:26:19,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, is one always aware of the background?

381
00:26:20,060 --> 00:26:20,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

382
00:26:21,520 --> 00:26:22,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's not something you attend to.

383
00:26:24,001 --> 00:26:25,641
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, one is always aware of the background.

384
00:26:25,962 --> 00:26:27,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, whichever the background is at the time.

385
00:26:29,642 --> 00:26:31,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the arahant is always mindful.

386
00:26:32,225 --> 00:26:33,246
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't have to be specific.

387
00:26:33,787 --> 00:26:34,267
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

388
00:26:34,688 --> 00:26:43,138
[SPEAKER_00]: People think... for as long as people think mindfulness is what you attend, then they can't comprehend how can arahant be always mindful, always mindful, and not be a robot at the same time.

389
00:26:44,245 --> 00:26:45,406
[SPEAKER_00]: No, this is how he's mindful.

390
00:26:45,726 --> 00:26:58,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Because his extent of his discernment has been developed so far now, that what we speak about, this significance of the beating heart, is a very low-level phenomena upon which you can establish your mind.

391
00:26:58,115 --> 00:27:00,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine you discern within that and within that.

392
00:27:01,137 --> 00:27:05,700
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is always something enduring, for as long as these aggregates don't break apart.

393
00:27:07,101 --> 00:27:07,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what we meant.

394
00:27:08,342 --> 00:27:12,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, he develops mindfulness to the extent necessary for the final knowledge.

395
00:27:13,932 --> 00:27:15,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, to the extent.

396
00:27:23,456 --> 00:27:34,041
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you can, like, as I said, all the all the things that were implied in the in the perception of the beating heart, that's not in your control, yet it determines your whole life, simultaneously, while you're alive.

397
00:27:36,942 --> 00:27:39,263
[SPEAKER_00]: You can go further within the same perception.

398
00:27:40,790 --> 00:27:41,970
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the characteristics?

399
00:27:42,050 --> 00:27:45,411
[SPEAKER_00]: What are the nimittas of that significance of that beating heart?

400
00:27:45,891 --> 00:27:47,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's like not my control.

401
00:27:48,251 --> 00:27:55,573
[SPEAKER_00]: So, my sense of control fundamentally depends upon the greater domain of non-control, of inaccessible to my control.

402
00:27:56,773 --> 00:27:58,153
[SPEAKER_00]: That's nothing other than anicca.

403
00:27:59,414 --> 00:28:02,094
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you develop the perception of impermanence, correctly.

404
00:28:03,154 --> 00:28:05,995
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be owned, it cannot be appropriate, it cannot be controlled.

405
00:28:06,115 --> 00:28:06,955
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impermanent.

406
00:28:14,859 --> 00:28:20,142
[SPEAKER_00]: The universal characteristics are the most universal.

407
00:28:20,742 --> 00:28:31,509
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said, if, you know, sometimes the mind just won't be, sort of, getting established upon, because it's too subtle and it got too distracted, whatever, you bring it down.

408
00:28:31,549 --> 00:28:35,771
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why they would have such a various range of different approaches.

409
00:28:35,952 --> 00:28:37,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Think about, look at the dead corpse.

410
00:28:39,454 --> 00:28:39,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah?

411
00:28:41,742 --> 00:28:51,910
[SPEAKER_00]: On some days, that will not be necessary, because it would be enough just to think about a phenomenon of non-control, and it would be fully establishing dispassion, renunciation, relinquishing, and everything else.

412
00:28:54,311 --> 00:28:55,392
[SPEAKER_00]: But on some days, you won't.

413
00:28:56,833 --> 00:29:00,496
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're... either way, you're developing the same principle.

414
00:29:03,158 --> 00:29:10,063
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to know that you are developing correctly, we've actually got somewhere in development.

415
00:29:11,662 --> 00:29:15,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the sign of dispassion is always there.

416
00:29:16,865 --> 00:29:19,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the sign of freedom from suffering.

417
00:29:20,828 --> 00:29:21,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Will always be there.

418
00:29:22,369 --> 00:29:23,690
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind is properly established, yeah.

419
00:29:23,710 --> 00:29:27,012
[SPEAKER_01]: In regards to everything that you're experiencing, that's... Why?

420
00:29:27,052 --> 00:29:35,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, you have shifted the emphasis from me and I onto the enduring phenomena that exists on its own.

421
00:29:36,556 --> 00:29:42,480
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even when the disagreeable things arise, it's like it becomes inconceivable to become involved in the personal manner.

422
00:29:42,520 --> 00:29:51,825
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just... the wind blows, you know, the branches fall, the sun shines, the heart beats, people shout abuse at you.

423
00:29:53,146 --> 00:29:54,427
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just... it's on the same level.

424
00:29:56,829 --> 00:30:03,753
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha listed it as like elements to be endured or enduring the malicious speech and hurtful speech and so on.

425
00:30:16,557 --> 00:30:17,197
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like the breath.

426
00:30:17,237 --> 00:30:18,738
[SPEAKER_01]: You can breathe.

427
00:30:19,619 --> 00:30:20,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Breathing is always there.

428
00:30:21,060 --> 00:30:21,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

429
00:30:21,640 --> 00:30:23,241
[SPEAKER_01]: The intention to breathe, maybe.

430
00:30:23,601 --> 00:30:24,622
[SPEAKER_01]: To be more specific.

431
00:30:25,602 --> 00:30:25,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

432
00:30:26,282 --> 00:30:27,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, just breathing.

433
00:30:27,363 --> 00:30:28,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Breathing is always there.

434
00:30:28,244 --> 00:30:29,104
[SPEAKER_00]: You can say that, yeah.

435
00:30:29,244 --> 00:30:34,487
[SPEAKER_01]: But I can sit now and do... play with my breath, maybe.

436
00:30:34,507 --> 00:30:35,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, okay.

437
00:30:36,448 --> 00:30:37,469
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to know.

438
00:30:38,169 --> 00:30:38,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Know this.

439
00:30:38,549 --> 00:30:39,230
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to start.

440
00:30:39,490 --> 00:30:41,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Like now, for most people,

441
00:30:41,741 --> 00:30:42,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

442
00:30:42,141 --> 00:30:45,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they're too concerned with what they attend.

443
00:30:45,865 --> 00:30:52,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the people who try to practice, breath is pretty much peripheral in regard to their other actions.

444
00:30:54,034 --> 00:30:55,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Breath goes into the background.

445
00:30:56,824 --> 00:31:02,708
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you bring the breath into the foreground, as in like, okay, I'm sitting here, breathing in, breathing out.

446
00:31:02,848 --> 00:31:04,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, in and out, breath.

447
00:31:04,249 --> 00:31:04,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

448
00:31:04,850 --> 00:31:08,312
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't do it as a sense of, I'm going to be attending every second of this.

449
00:31:08,672 --> 00:31:13,055
[SPEAKER_00]: You bring it to the foreground, so that you will see the background of the background of the breath.

450
00:31:14,857 --> 00:31:17,919
[SPEAKER_00]: For most of the day-to-day experience, breath is in the background.

451
00:31:20,300 --> 00:31:21,861
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, you can establish significance.

452
00:31:22,422 --> 00:31:23,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Will you be alive if the breathing stops?

453
00:31:25,251 --> 00:31:26,432
[SPEAKER_01]: If your airways get blocked.

454
00:31:26,692 --> 00:31:26,872
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

455
00:31:28,353 --> 00:31:35,719
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could establish the same principle as we just did, of dispassion, relinquishment, non-control, not-self, by seeing the background of your breath.

456
00:31:36,400 --> 00:31:42,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, you can, why don't I refine it further, and start seeing the background of the actual act of breathing.

457
00:31:43,245 --> 00:31:45,386
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how ānāpānasati sutta goes.

458
00:31:46,287 --> 00:31:48,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Which says, knowingly, he breathes in.

459
00:31:49,289 --> 00:31:50,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowingly, he breathes out.

460
00:31:51,691 --> 00:31:53,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, I'm breathing in, I'm breathing in, I'm breathing in, I'm breathing...

461
00:31:54,644 --> 00:31:56,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Now I'm breathing out, breathing out, breathing out, breathing out.

462
00:31:56,525 --> 00:31:56,785
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

463
00:31:56,985 --> 00:31:59,186
[SPEAKER_00]: He's breathing in, breathing out, and he knows it.

464
00:31:59,727 --> 00:32:03,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, now he's discerning even more general background to that.

465
00:32:04,029 --> 00:32:06,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that... that's what's... what's in front of him.

466
00:32:06,711 --> 00:32:09,972
[SPEAKER_00]: He puts his mindfulness to the fore, as the Sutta says.

467
00:32:11,433 --> 00:32:12,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, I'm sitting here.

468
00:32:12,654 --> 00:32:16,656
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not doing anything else, except breathing.

469
00:32:21,067 --> 00:32:22,128
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not controlling it.

470
00:32:22,348 --> 00:32:23,148
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm just doing it.

471
00:32:23,748 --> 00:32:25,069
[SPEAKER_00]: I can do it like this, or I can do...

472
00:32:25,089 --> 00:32:25,669
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

473
00:32:26,029 --> 00:32:29,251
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is knowing the background of that doing.

474
00:32:30,512 --> 00:32:33,533
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you establish mindfulness of the breathing.

475
00:32:34,133 --> 00:32:36,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness is synonymous, basically, with background.

476
00:32:36,915 --> 00:32:39,136
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you say mindfulness, you say background.

477
00:32:39,316 --> 00:32:41,797
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can call mindfulness of breathing as background of the breathing.

478
00:32:43,738 --> 00:32:45,019
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's the background of the breathing?

479
00:32:46,852 --> 00:32:49,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, fundamentally, it comes down to the knowledge of action.

480
00:32:50,194 --> 00:32:50,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Any action.

481
00:32:51,094 --> 00:32:52,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Because breathing is an action.

482
00:32:52,875 --> 00:32:54,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that would be, like, if you fully discern it.

483
00:32:56,597 --> 00:33:03,940
[SPEAKER_00]: But what is, what is, like, initially, what it would be, the background of the breathing, is that knowledge of breathing.

484
00:33:03,960 --> 00:33:06,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowledge of the necessity of breath, the necessity of the body.

485
00:33:07,382 --> 00:33:10,723
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, when I say necessity of those things, means you are not necessary.

486
00:33:10,803 --> 00:33:11,884
[SPEAKER_00]: These things are necessary.

487
00:33:12,924 --> 00:33:14,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like narrowing down.

488
00:33:16,195 --> 00:33:20,638
[SPEAKER_00]: The field of your concern, like, I must do this, I must do that, and you get distracted.

489
00:33:20,738 --> 00:33:21,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can't get distracted.

490
00:33:22,599 --> 00:33:31,645
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it says, oh, you develop this, and the body you're discerning there, is that the body, is the body among the bodies, as in, is the body that stands for the phenomenon of the body at the time.

491
00:33:43,077 --> 00:33:48,840
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you use your breathing basically as like a natural confinement for the concern of your thought.

492
00:33:50,221 --> 00:33:54,123
[SPEAKER_00]: And you become mindful of it properly when you start discerning the background of that confinement.

493
00:33:55,063 --> 00:34:03,947
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's... you cannot... as I said, you discern the confinement because breathing is already there, whether you attend to it or not.

494
00:34:04,428 --> 00:34:06,989
[SPEAKER_00]: You certainly don't make a mechanical method out of it.

495
00:34:07,829 --> 00:34:09,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Watch my nostril, watch my nostril, watch my nostril.

496
00:34:10,091 --> 00:34:10,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there's no background.

497
00:34:11,301 --> 00:34:11,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

498
00:34:11,921 --> 00:34:17,645
[SPEAKER_00]: You fully just... Well, there is a background, but you're actively looking away, you're actively trying to focus on what's in front of you.

499
00:34:20,967 --> 00:34:26,870
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what about, as example, like, when you learn something, 2 plus 2 equals 4.

500
00:34:29,011 --> 00:34:30,372
[SPEAKER_01]: You know that now.

501
00:34:31,513 --> 00:34:33,474
[SPEAKER_01]: So, every time you look at 2 plus 2...

502
00:34:33,814 --> 00:34:34,554
[SPEAKER_00]: The knowledge is there.

503
00:34:34,695 --> 00:34:34,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

504
00:34:35,035 --> 00:34:35,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

505
00:34:36,003 --> 00:34:40,225
[SPEAKER_00]: So, imagine the same principle applied to the universal nature of experience.

506
00:34:40,805 --> 00:34:47,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever you look at anything that has arisen, you see its cessation, as the inside of sotāpanna.

507
00:34:47,148 --> 00:34:53,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever has the nature of arising, not whatever has arisen in front of me, it's impermanent.

508
00:34:53,091 --> 00:34:56,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever has the nature of arising means it's only the peripheral knowledge.

509
00:34:56,692 --> 00:34:58,693
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature is something you cannot attend.

510
00:34:59,754 --> 00:35:01,875
[SPEAKER_00]: You can discern based on things you're attending.

511
00:35:02,632 --> 00:35:10,458
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you discern that whatever it has the nature of arising, for that reason, has to cease.

512
00:35:11,199 --> 00:35:15,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever has the nature... whatever has arisen has the sign of... cessation.

513
00:35:15,663 --> 00:35:17,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of its arising.

514
00:35:17,484 --> 00:35:20,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because it's going to disappear, but because it has arisen.

515
00:35:20,747 --> 00:35:26,892
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever things arise for you, which is for as long as the five aggregates exist, means you see impermanence.

516
00:35:27,272 --> 00:35:29,114
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why arahantship is irreversible.

517
00:35:29,214 --> 00:35:30,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Right view is irreversible.

518
00:35:31,985 --> 00:35:34,667
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot forget that, because everything contains it.

519
00:35:34,907 --> 00:35:35,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything.

520
00:35:37,789 --> 00:35:41,812
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's such a general statement, because that's the most accurate way you could say it.

521
00:35:42,372 --> 00:35:46,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever has the nature of arising, has the nature of ceasing, because of that.

522
00:35:46,155 --> 00:35:46,535
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

523
00:35:50,057 --> 00:35:57,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So that significance, that sign, is always there, because whatever is manifested,

524
00:36:00,938 --> 00:36:01,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

525
00:36:01,819 --> 00:36:02,981
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is its nature.

526
00:36:03,001 --> 00:36:13,374
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the dhamma, as in the phenomena, and the dhamma, as in the teaching, is like the nature of... it's the phenomena, the phenomenology, literally, the knowledge of the phenomena.

527
00:36:14,936 --> 00:36:17,539
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever has the nature of manifestation,

528
00:36:18,580 --> 00:36:22,362
[SPEAKER_00]: It has the nature of this teaching of the dhamma, as in the nature of impermanence and so on.

529
00:36:22,382 --> 00:36:24,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the most general and the most...

530
00:36:25,084 --> 00:36:26,125
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the most fundamental.

531
00:36:26,165 --> 00:36:27,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, look at the breath.

532
00:36:27,686 --> 00:36:29,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Or now, contemplate the body.

533
00:36:29,147 --> 00:36:31,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, contemplate the six senses.

534
00:36:31,868 --> 00:36:41,915
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you look at manifestation... Well, no, you look at the breath, you contemplate the body, so to keep discerning this nature of manifestation clearer and clearer.

535
00:36:42,115 --> 00:36:42,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

536
00:36:43,536 --> 00:36:44,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the more...

537
00:36:45,988 --> 00:36:50,151
[SPEAKER_00]: When I say nature manifestation, I mean what I said when we were establishing the significance of the beating heart.

538
00:36:50,611 --> 00:36:52,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Already there on its own, manifested.

539
00:36:53,693 --> 00:36:55,174
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not creating it by attending it.

540
00:36:55,294 --> 00:36:57,876
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only attend it because it's already there, enduring.

541
00:37:00,258 --> 00:37:04,321
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot create anything from nothingness into, like, bring it into being.

542
00:37:05,381 --> 00:37:06,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you are the result of it.

543
00:37:08,564 --> 00:37:11,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Your attention is already secondary to it, structurally.

544
00:37:11,946 --> 00:37:13,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Not as one, two, three.

545
00:37:14,740 --> 00:37:21,386
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all like within the same arisen basis of the present form paired with consciousness, that you cannot step outside of.

546
00:37:21,466 --> 00:37:23,768
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any movement you make within it, it's within it.

547
00:37:24,589 --> 00:37:29,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you trying to attend that, would require you to step outside of it and see it as an object.

548
00:37:29,073 --> 00:37:30,134
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's inconceivable.

549
00:37:30,694 --> 00:37:33,697
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you keep doing it, it's because you're misconceiving that you can do it.

550
00:37:35,138 --> 00:37:35,719
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

551
00:37:35,739 --> 00:37:36,680
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to discern.

552
00:37:38,668 --> 00:37:46,371
[SPEAKER_00]: These universal characteristics, onto things, not onto you attending and they exist only when I attend to them.

553
00:37:46,431 --> 00:37:48,311
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you uproot your sense of self.

554
00:37:48,992 --> 00:37:55,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you can't step outside of yourself, or the five aggregates, but you can certainly stop misconceiving them, but that can be done only from the inside.

555
00:37:57,135 --> 00:38:03,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's, you know, a bhikkhu... How is a bhikkhu a long-distance shooter, anyway?

556
00:38:03,697 --> 00:38:03,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh-huh.

557
00:38:04,777 --> 00:38:12,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, any kind of form whatsoever, past, future, present, whatever, big receives all form as it really is with correct wisdom.

558
00:38:13,040 --> 00:38:13,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

559
00:38:13,500 --> 00:38:15,201
[SPEAKER_00]: So, shooting the peripheral discernment.

560
00:38:15,781 --> 00:38:32,287
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean, like, the significance of the heart, then, within that, you can discern what's further, what's more refined significance that this beating heart already implies, which is the significance of earth, significance of water, fire, significance of impermanence.

561
00:38:34,419 --> 00:38:35,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Significance of non-control.

562
00:38:37,440 --> 00:38:38,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

563
00:38:38,181 --> 00:38:52,887
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if Bhikkhu is a long-distance shooter, is if he doesn't even have to anymore go through, like, established significance of the heart to discern these other more refined significances, his mind is already developed to the level of discerning refined significances in whatever it has arisen.

564
00:38:52,907 --> 00:38:59,970
[SPEAKER_00]: And those refined significances, the refined signs, are the impermanence, four great elements, or whatever.

565
00:39:03,398 --> 00:39:11,002
[SPEAKER_00]: It goes for the significances that are fundamental to freedom from suffering, basically.

566
00:39:12,663 --> 00:39:12,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it?

567
00:39:13,223 --> 00:39:13,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

568
00:39:13,543 --> 00:39:14,244
[SPEAKER_01]: This is suffering.

569
00:39:14,844 --> 00:39:15,304
[SPEAKER_00]: There you go.

570
00:39:15,464 --> 00:39:15,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

571
00:39:15,625 --> 00:39:17,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's all that's all it matters.

572
00:39:17,085 --> 00:39:17,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

573
00:39:17,566 --> 00:39:18,626
[SPEAKER_00]: You would arrive at that.

574
00:39:19,167 --> 00:39:19,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Which sutra is that?

575
00:39:19,787 --> 00:39:20,247
[SPEAKER_00]: What is that?

576
00:39:20,807 --> 00:39:21,548
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the warrior.

577
00:39:28,792 --> 00:39:35,736
[SPEAKER_00]: You would arrive at that through contemplating the independence of the great matter, your dependence on it, and so on.

578
00:39:36,176 --> 00:39:41,279
[SPEAKER_00]: You would eventually drain away any conceit, any misconceiving, which would result in freedom from suffering.

579
00:39:41,439 --> 00:39:49,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, if you should precisely, yeah, you go straight for freedom from suffering, because that's already implied.

580
00:39:51,123 --> 00:40:06,392
[SPEAKER_00]: That's also how, like, Paññavimutti, one liberated through wisdom, when he discerns only things relevant to liberation.

581
00:40:08,353 --> 00:40:10,955
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't develop, like, all the jhānas and everything else.

582
00:40:13,717 --> 00:40:22,224
[SPEAKER_00]: See, like now, when you speak about it, for most people who listen to this, it would be quite an abstract thing.

583
00:40:23,465 --> 00:40:27,488
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only reason it's abstract is because the mind hasn't been developed in regard to that abstraction.

584
00:40:27,528 --> 00:40:28,548
[SPEAKER_00]: So, start developing it.

585
00:40:29,849 --> 00:40:36,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Start discerning it in arisen things that endure for you, in front of you, throughout your daily life.

586
00:40:36,714 --> 00:40:38,216
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... because these things are there.

587
00:40:38,796 --> 00:40:41,158
[SPEAKER_00]: They will remain abstract for as long as you don't see where they are.

588
00:40:43,962 --> 00:40:51,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you abstract that, literally.

589
00:40:51,348 --> 00:40:55,472
[SPEAKER_01]: How is a bhikkhu one who splits a great body?

590
00:40:55,492 --> 00:41:00,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, a bhikkhu splits the great mass of ignorance.

591
00:41:00,516 --> 00:41:04,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Practicing these, yeah, these four qualities, he's worthy of offerings.

592
00:41:06,561 --> 00:41:09,764
[SPEAKER_01]: And how is a bhikkhu skilled in places?

593
00:41:09,784 --> 00:41:11,005
[SPEAKER_01]: He's virtuous.

594
00:41:21,750 --> 00:41:23,511
[SPEAKER_00]: That's also where you see the danger.

595
00:41:24,632 --> 00:41:26,094
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in that peripheral.

596
00:41:27,615 --> 00:41:28,916
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is in the background.

597
00:41:29,196 --> 00:41:31,678
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is the implication of what's in front of you.

598
00:41:32,599 --> 00:41:34,821
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is the context that you create.

599
00:41:37,703 --> 00:41:39,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Like Putujana doesn't see the danger, essentially.

600
00:41:40,285 --> 00:41:44,988
[SPEAKER_00]: But if he starts thinking about it, discerning it, he'll get to see it because it is actually dangerous.

601
00:41:46,809 --> 00:41:53,313
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you can develop the danger in the slightest fault and not become neurotically obsessed about every little thing that's in front of you, fearing it.

602
00:41:54,294 --> 00:41:56,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's not where the fear... that's not where the danger is.

603
00:41:56,956 --> 00:42:00,098
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the simile of the beautiful, tasty beverage.

604
00:42:00,798 --> 00:42:03,140
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't see the poison in it.

605
00:42:04,280 --> 00:42:05,141
[SPEAKER_00]: But you know it.

606
00:42:06,185 --> 00:42:15,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, ultimately, the danger of sensuality is rooted in the background and never... can never come in front of you and be seen in the same manner you see object of your senses.

607
00:42:16,309 --> 00:42:23,032
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people are intoxicated with sensuality, despite the experiences always proving that it's actually painful.

608
00:42:24,393 --> 00:42:31,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you only regard things as real, as the things that you can see through your senses, usually.

609
00:42:31,376 --> 00:42:34,517
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to discern... and that danger, as I said, never leaves the peripheral.

610
00:42:35,638 --> 00:42:38,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Never becomes like, oh, yeah, I see the danger with my eyes.

611
00:42:38,321 --> 00:42:38,481
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

612
00:42:38,541 --> 00:42:39,863
[SPEAKER_00]: You still see the beautiful object.

613
00:42:40,383 --> 00:42:44,147
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, you have fully understood and you fully discern the danger.

614
00:42:44,668 --> 00:42:47,691
[SPEAKER_00]: The significance of the enduring peripheral object...

615
00:42:47,972 --> 00:42:50,975
[SPEAKER_00]: The significance of the enduring beautiful object is the peripheral danger.

616
00:42:51,215 --> 00:42:51,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

617
00:42:52,637 --> 00:42:53,217
[SPEAKER_00]: It is it.

618
00:42:54,058 --> 00:42:54,799
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not maybe.

619
00:42:55,320 --> 00:42:56,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Or like danger will come after.

620
00:42:56,701 --> 00:42:57,122
[SPEAKER_00]: It's it.

621
00:42:59,047 --> 00:43:05,252
[SPEAKER_00]: The beverage, the drink, that contains poison that will kill you, you can't see it, you can't smell it, it's undetectable.

622
00:43:05,753 --> 00:43:06,853
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the Buddha simile.

623
00:43:06,913 --> 00:43:08,935
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not making this up.

624
00:43:09,075 --> 00:43:10,556
[SPEAKER_00]: The poison is undetectable.

625
00:43:13,979 --> 00:43:17,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless you know there's poison there, you'll be drinking it.

626
00:43:18,662 --> 00:43:19,443
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what people do.

627
00:43:20,524 --> 00:43:22,665
[SPEAKER_00]: So you have to know that poison is there.

628
00:43:23,026 --> 00:43:25,548
[SPEAKER_00]: And you have to stop ignoring the fact that poison is there.

629
00:43:26,028 --> 00:43:26,949
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you get to see.

630
00:43:28,305 --> 00:43:31,408
[SPEAKER_00]: That whatever is beautiful, yeah, you fully understood.

631
00:43:32,829 --> 00:43:33,590
[SPEAKER_00]: This is dangerous.

632
00:43:33,670 --> 00:43:34,290
[SPEAKER_00]: This will kill me.

633
00:43:34,831 --> 00:43:35,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Poison is there.

634
00:43:36,112 --> 00:43:37,133
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't see the poison.

635
00:43:37,153 --> 00:43:39,535
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't extract it and see like, look, this is the poison.

636
00:43:39,555 --> 00:43:39,735
[SPEAKER_00]: See?

637
00:43:40,455 --> 00:43:43,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that would then imply that other things you can see don't have that poison.

638
00:43:44,699 --> 00:43:47,501
[SPEAKER_00]: So, poison is in everything you see, hear, smell, taste, touch.

639
00:43:48,482 --> 00:43:49,443
[SPEAKER_00]: In everything that's agreeable.

640
00:43:49,743 --> 00:43:50,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Contains the poison.

641
00:43:50,864 --> 00:43:51,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's agreeable.

642
00:43:53,086 --> 00:43:56,049
[SPEAKER_00]: You could think that agreeability of things, it's what poison is.

643
00:43:57,147 --> 00:43:58,848
[SPEAKER_00]: But that in itself, it's not dangerous, isn't it?

644
00:43:59,408 --> 00:44:09,431
[SPEAKER_00]: What's dangerous is you carelessly ingesting the poison, you carelessly welcoming the agreeability, delighting in it, saying yes to it.

645
00:44:09,951 --> 00:44:11,431
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the danger is not the result.

646
00:44:12,071 --> 00:44:13,492
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, exactly, exactly.

647
00:44:13,552 --> 00:44:15,472
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is that significance of it.

648
00:44:16,613 --> 00:44:20,594
[SPEAKER_00]: If you take it, then that danger befalls you.

649
00:44:21,494 --> 00:44:23,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's dangerous to go to those forests.

650
00:44:23,615 --> 00:44:24,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you don't go.

651
00:44:25,609 --> 00:44:29,570
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's dangerous to go to a forest, and it's like, well, you know, I'll be fine.

652
00:44:29,630 --> 00:44:30,270
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you go.

653
00:44:30,470 --> 00:44:30,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

654
00:44:31,531 --> 00:44:38,733
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because, you know, what we usually say, danger of sensuality is, if you overindulge... Yeah, exactly, exactly.

655
00:44:38,853 --> 00:44:45,875
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no,

656
00:44:47,460 --> 00:44:47,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

657
00:44:48,020 --> 00:44:54,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, like, I suppose, if somebody is completely unrestrained, has to start, like, rounding it up from such a crude manner.

658
00:44:54,222 --> 00:44:55,082
[SPEAKER_00]: I lose my money.

659
00:44:55,362 --> 00:44:55,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

660
00:44:55,562 --> 00:44:56,582
[SPEAKER_00]: I lose my friend.

661
00:44:56,622 --> 00:44:56,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

662
00:44:56,922 --> 00:45:02,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are reflections that complete asuta or putujana will have to go through in order to begin.

663
00:45:02,904 --> 00:45:03,904
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not the danger.

664
00:45:03,924 --> 00:45:08,345
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha would say, you're not free from saṃsārtha until the danger becomes apparent.

665
00:45:09,670 --> 00:45:14,333
[SPEAKER_00]: And the danger has not become apparent if you only wait for you to even get a sense of danger.

666
00:45:14,353 --> 00:45:19,516
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to get through the motion of repeating how dissatisfying sensuality actually is.

667
00:45:19,596 --> 00:45:21,397
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's on the level of psychology.

668
00:45:21,417 --> 00:45:22,557
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like rationalizing.

669
00:45:23,478 --> 00:45:26,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it can, as I said, it can help somebody who has absolutely no ground for practice.

670
00:45:27,793 --> 00:45:30,154
[SPEAKER_00]: But you want to go way beyond that.

671
00:45:30,314 --> 00:45:34,796
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to be able... seeing the agreeable equals seeing the danger.

672
00:45:34,816 --> 00:45:38,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Although you can't see the danger with your eyes, seeing the agreeable equals knowing the danger.

673
00:45:39,358 --> 00:45:43,239
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Buddha said knowledge comes... knowledge is secondary to perception.

674
00:45:45,360 --> 00:45:49,382
[SPEAKER_00]: But yet, you can equally have perception without knowledge being present there.

675
00:45:50,342 --> 00:45:54,105
[SPEAKER_00]: So, knowledge is not in perception, which means you can't perceive the knowledge.

676
00:45:54,565 --> 00:45:58,548
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you can know that which you perceive at the same time.

677
00:46:00,369 --> 00:46:06,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, a cup of drink in front of you, and you're drinking it, and it's very good.

678
00:46:06,994 --> 00:46:08,034
[SPEAKER_00]: People told you it's healthy.

679
00:46:08,715 --> 00:46:10,976
[SPEAKER_00]: People told you it's going to prolong your life and cure cancer.

680
00:46:11,977 --> 00:46:12,658
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, wow!

681
00:46:13,398 --> 00:46:15,860
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, somebody comes and tell you,

682
00:46:17,612 --> 00:46:20,213
[SPEAKER_00]: That it's actually gonna kill you, gonna shorten your life.

683
00:46:22,473 --> 00:46:26,155
[SPEAKER_00]: See, both could be right, as in you don't know which one's right.

684
00:46:26,735 --> 00:46:32,917
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact now that it has been placed into doubt, that it's good for you, it's actually enough for you to back off from it.

685
00:46:33,217 --> 00:46:34,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Or better not take any chances.

686
00:46:35,577 --> 00:46:36,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Perhaps I don't need a longer life.

687
00:46:39,358 --> 00:46:41,259
[SPEAKER_00]: As long... because, you know, it might actually shorten it.

688
00:46:42,539 --> 00:46:47,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Let alone, if it actually becomes apparent that, no, the second guy was right, it will kill you quicker.

689
00:46:48,200 --> 00:46:48,860
[SPEAKER_00]: It will ruin you.

690
00:46:49,681 --> 00:46:52,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the danger...

691
00:46:52,601 --> 00:46:54,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, the cup, the content, nothing has changed.

692
00:46:55,262 --> 00:46:56,062
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all in front of you.

693
00:46:56,102 --> 00:46:57,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Your experience is always still the same.

694
00:46:57,322 --> 00:46:57,682
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in might.

695
00:46:59,003 --> 00:46:59,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

696
00:46:59,503 --> 00:47:01,703
[SPEAKER_00]: You start discerning the danger even in might.

697
00:47:02,723 --> 00:47:03,344
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I'm saying.

698
00:47:05,424 --> 00:47:09,265
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that you don't know for yourself that it's good for you, that's already dangerous.

699
00:47:10,082 --> 00:47:13,063
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you start thinking like that, the danger will become apparent.

700
00:47:13,403 --> 00:47:15,324
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger that is actually there.

701
00:47:16,004 --> 00:47:18,145
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't see that until you arrive at it.

702
00:47:18,905 --> 00:47:20,386
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you can't know that for yourself.

703
00:47:20,446 --> 00:47:24,787
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to take it on trust, that sensuality is dangerous.

704
00:47:24,807 --> 00:47:26,088
[SPEAKER_00]: And start regarding it as such.

705
00:47:26,188 --> 00:47:32,910
[SPEAKER_00]: And because it is dangerous, your way of regarding it will eventually mirror how it is, and you'll get to understand it.

706
00:47:33,410 --> 00:47:38,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sensuality as in, you want to see sensuality.

707
00:47:42,490 --> 00:47:43,870
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to see it in front of you.

708
00:47:44,251 --> 00:47:44,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality.

709
00:47:45,051 --> 00:47:45,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

710
00:47:45,931 --> 00:47:46,912
[SPEAKER_00]: The domain of sensuality.

711
00:47:46,932 --> 00:47:48,392
[SPEAKER_00]: The domain of sensual attraction.

712
00:47:49,172 --> 00:47:54,294
[SPEAKER_00]: The domain of seeking pleasure on the grounds of your own body.

713
00:47:55,234 --> 00:47:58,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Smell, taste, sight, smell, taste, touch, and so on.

714
00:47:58,455 --> 00:48:00,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Domain or option of sensuality.

715
00:48:00,336 --> 00:48:01,617
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is sensuality, yeah.

716
00:48:01,877 --> 00:48:08,459
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, the peripheral of that perception of sensuality is the knowledge of the danger.

717
00:48:08,479 --> 00:48:10,039
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the significance of the danger.

718
00:48:10,139 --> 00:48:11,540
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the implication of the danger.

719
00:48:17,809 --> 00:48:19,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Especially if you don't have the knowledge of the danger.

720
00:48:19,830 --> 00:48:20,750
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you looking for?

721
00:48:20,910 --> 00:48:25,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, first, if you don't... if you don't discern the phenomenon of sensuality, you're not gonna...

722
00:48:26,251 --> 00:48:34,094
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, as I said, mindfulness of breathing, it's like a confinement that represents all the other actions you do in your life on account of your breathing.

723
00:48:35,194 --> 00:48:37,555
[SPEAKER_00]: And... but then, you're not doing that confinement.

724
00:48:37,575 --> 00:48:39,755
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to discern the background of that confinement.

725
00:48:40,696 --> 00:48:43,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, that makes everything inferior to that, even clearer.

726
00:48:43,576 --> 00:48:45,877
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the same sense, you want to discern the extent

727
00:48:46,513 --> 00:48:56,096
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha would always start and say the gratification of sensuality has been understood.

728
00:48:56,716 --> 00:48:58,757
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger has become apparent.

729
00:48:58,797 --> 00:48:59,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

730
00:48:59,277 --> 00:49:01,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like the danger has become apparent and then I understand.

731
00:49:01,937 --> 00:49:02,098
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

732
00:49:02,358 --> 00:49:10,320
[SPEAKER_00]: By understanding the extent of sensuality, by understanding its gratification, then the background of that, the danger becomes clear.

733
00:49:11,289 --> 00:49:18,693
[SPEAKER_00]: But not before you understand the extent of, basically, physical pleasures, craving for physical pleasures.

734
00:49:19,954 --> 00:49:23,016
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can only understand the extent if you start looking for the extent.

735
00:49:23,556 --> 00:49:28,159
[SPEAKER_00]: And the extent, the gratification of sensuality, is not in the particular engagement with this sensuality.

736
00:49:28,239 --> 00:49:32,781
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in the background of your particular engagement.

737
00:49:33,001 --> 00:49:34,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

738
00:49:34,162 --> 00:49:35,103
[SPEAKER_01]: All the possibilities.

739
00:49:35,803 --> 00:49:36,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

740
00:49:36,243 --> 00:49:40,085
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by saying no to the particular engagement of sensuality... What?

741
00:49:40,166 --> 00:49:40,646
[SPEAKER_00]: No, sorry.

742
00:49:41,313 --> 00:49:51,595
[SPEAKER_00]: There are obviously sensualities that you cannot be mindful of, the ones that take the mind too far out, such as non-celibacy, killing, lying, stealing.

743
00:49:51,735 --> 00:49:52,755
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you abandon that.

744
00:49:53,275 --> 00:49:54,676
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, you have to eat.

745
00:49:55,276 --> 00:49:57,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Within that, you're going to get pleasant taste in your mouth.

746
00:49:57,796 --> 00:50:00,217
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's like, oh, I must spit this out because it's too pleasant.

747
00:50:00,777 --> 00:50:00,937
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

748
00:50:01,257 --> 00:50:02,717
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see the gratification there.

749
00:50:03,257 --> 00:50:04,018
[SPEAKER_00]: You fear everything.

750
00:50:04,038 --> 00:50:04,878
[SPEAKER_00]: You're spitting the object.

751
00:50:04,898 --> 00:50:06,178
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not what sensuality is.

752
00:50:06,723 --> 00:50:09,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality is your attitude in regard to the object you're attending.

753
00:50:10,109 --> 00:50:14,276
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if all you do is attend to things, you're never gonna see your attitude in regard to it.

754
00:50:15,779 --> 00:50:20,326
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now you live restrained, you're not breaking vinaya, you see the danger in the slightest fault,

755
00:50:21,087 --> 00:50:25,830
[SPEAKER_00]: When you encounter agreeable things that are based for sensuality, that's when you start looking for the background of that.

756
00:50:26,250 --> 00:50:27,371
[SPEAKER_00]: You allow it to arise.

757
00:50:27,851 --> 00:50:28,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the taste is in my mouth.

758
00:50:28,892 --> 00:50:30,173
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha doesn't say, spit the food.

759
00:50:30,573 --> 00:50:32,134
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, no, eat mindfully.

760
00:50:32,534 --> 00:50:37,357
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, eat remain... eat while you're aware of the background of your pleasant taste.

761
00:50:38,558 --> 00:50:40,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't get absorbed with the pleasant taste.

762
00:50:40,959 --> 00:50:47,303
[SPEAKER_00]: And people try to not get absorbed with pleasant taste by, you know, focusing on other things or avoiding or twisting their tongue to avoid the taste and whatnot.

763
00:50:48,283 --> 00:50:50,625
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's equally on the same footing where the taste is.

764
00:50:51,760 --> 00:50:54,302
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to just know the background of the pleasant taste.

765
00:50:55,143 --> 00:50:57,725
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already clarifying the extent of gratification.

766
00:50:58,907 --> 00:51:05,152
[SPEAKER_00]: The clearer the extent of gratification, the perception of gratification becomes, the significance of the danger becomes clearer.

767
00:51:06,614 --> 00:51:15,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the gratification became clear, the danger has been seen, has become apparent, and then the way out from sensuality and gratification is automatic.

768
00:51:26,248 --> 00:51:33,357
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to see the extent, to see the extent, the possibilities, whatever you experience... First, you have to be restrained.

769
00:51:34,607 --> 00:51:35,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Not give in to distraction.

770
00:51:35,768 --> 00:51:42,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you intentionally give in to sensuality, yeah, you can't establish that mindfulness, because your intention already distorted it.

771
00:51:42,713 --> 00:51:44,614
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already inverted, perverted.

772
00:51:45,375 --> 00:51:57,623
[SPEAKER_00]: But if agreeable things that are based for sensuality come to you, without your intention necessarily being rooted in seeking the pleasure on, like, in them, that's where you start discerning the peripheral.

773
00:51:57,644 --> 00:52:00,425
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the gratification of sensuality becomes clear.

774
00:52:01,386 --> 00:52:02,927
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why sīla has to come first.

775
00:52:02,987 --> 00:52:03,868
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just not optional.

776
00:52:08,203 --> 00:52:18,505
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's what I mean, like, the problem is when people... sīla comes first, and then the denial of any anything pleasant that comes, even if it's not seeked by you, becomes the next attitude.

777
00:52:20,025 --> 00:52:24,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I want this... like, this taste is way too nice, so I only eat things that I hate.

778
00:52:25,126 --> 00:52:28,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you're basically misconceiving sensuality.

779
00:52:28,687 --> 00:52:33,208
[SPEAKER_00]: You think sensuality is in the actual taste, and you think that by avoiding taste, you avoid sensuality.

780
00:52:33,268 --> 00:52:33,428
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

781
00:52:33,528 --> 00:52:34,888
[SPEAKER_00]: You just obscure it further.

782
00:52:41,535 --> 00:52:49,342
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're free from sensuality, free from it, you would be what, eh... what would you be?

783
00:52:52,144 --> 00:52:57,569
[SPEAKER_00]: You would have... if you had right view... And free from sensuality, you'd be anāgāmī.

784
00:53:01,392 --> 00:53:01,733
[SPEAKER_01]: But if...

785
00:53:02,483 --> 00:53:09,029
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't have the right view, you could be free from sensuality and then you would need very, very little instruction to get the right view.

786
00:53:09,049 --> 00:53:12,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you've already done 80% of the work.

787
00:53:12,472 --> 00:53:18,557
[SPEAKER_00]: 80% of the fundamental existential distraction, which is the senses, have been tamed and surmounted and understood.

788
00:53:19,497 --> 00:53:25,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you see things as whatever has arisen, the background is it will cease.

789
00:53:25,523 --> 00:53:25,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

790
00:53:26,904 --> 00:53:30,487
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you turn your mind to what is the extent of sensuality?

791
00:53:31,123 --> 00:53:31,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

792
00:53:31,563 --> 00:53:32,644
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the same principle.

793
00:53:33,104 --> 00:53:44,208
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, those who don't see that principle, but have seen the sensuality, they have removed, pretty much, the sole reason for not seeing impermanence.

794
00:53:44,748 --> 00:53:47,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, virtually, sensuality is the main reason.

795
00:53:47,749 --> 00:53:50,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sensuality results in distraction and everything else.

796
00:53:52,171 --> 00:53:53,491
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have removed that.

797
00:53:53,731 --> 00:53:54,851
[SPEAKER_00]: You have understood that.

798
00:53:54,911 --> 00:53:56,612
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop your mind in regard to it.

799
00:53:56,712 --> 00:53:59,093
[SPEAKER_00]: You have seen the peripheral of that domain.

800
00:53:59,680 --> 00:54:11,384
[SPEAKER_00]: You've seen the danger in it, and then you just need somebody to tell you what the impermanence of phenomena is, and you will be... like, well, you wouldn't... you would immediately go straight to anāgāmī, basically, or even arahant.

801
00:54:12,745 --> 00:54:12,945
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

802
00:54:13,105 --> 00:54:14,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you've already done all the work.

803
00:54:15,666 --> 00:54:27,690
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you say that, sort of, the danger of sensuality is being, eh... is being pulled in,

804
00:54:29,771 --> 00:54:32,072
[SPEAKER_01]: You're in the foreground.

805
00:54:32,912 --> 00:54:33,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

806
00:54:33,872 --> 00:54:43,415
[SPEAKER_00]: See, all that is accurate, but you want to see that now, not as a statement that you made, but you actually want to see it coming from that sensuality.

807
00:54:45,936 --> 00:54:52,037
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to see it, yeah, you have to be clarifying that understanding, even those statements for yourself.

808
00:54:52,777 --> 00:54:58,039
[SPEAKER_00]: The more you clarify it, the more of the significance is being clarified, and then you will get to recognize it.

809
00:55:00,840 --> 00:55:04,061
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fundamental danger of sensuality is exactly that.

810
00:55:04,381 --> 00:55:12,982
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot remain not pulled, not distorted by the foreground.

811
00:55:13,602 --> 00:55:14,102
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot.

812
00:55:14,422 --> 00:55:15,483
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I wanted to say.

813
00:55:16,463 --> 00:55:24,384
[SPEAKER_01]: That the danger of sensuality is... and I was going to say it, but I thought maybe I was slightly wrong saying this.

814
00:55:24,424 --> 00:55:28,245
[SPEAKER_01]: The result of sensuality is the fact that you are

815
00:55:29,657 --> 00:55:30,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure.

816
00:55:30,197 --> 00:55:35,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that is like a cumulative result of sort of not abandoning sensuality and engaging with it.

817
00:55:57,386 --> 00:56:03,487
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger is being lost, that you will be... Well, the danger is not being free.

818
00:56:04,228 --> 00:56:06,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, lost in ignorance, not free.

819
00:56:07,628 --> 00:56:10,849
[SPEAKER_00]: You're only free when you surmount the danger.

820
00:56:10,929 --> 00:56:13,010
[SPEAKER_00]: You're only free when you surmount ignorance.

821
00:56:15,370 --> 00:56:16,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't surmount it, you're not free.

822
00:56:17,111 --> 00:56:19,391
[SPEAKER_00]: And that in itself is... that's the danger of saṃsāra.

823
00:56:19,991 --> 00:56:21,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Not free means saṃsāra.

824
00:56:22,592 --> 00:56:26,953
[SPEAKER_01]: So, am I not... is that not then... am I not then seeing the danger in such a way?

825
00:56:27,608 --> 00:56:31,030
[SPEAKER_00]: If I see that... Well, you are seeing to some extent, sure.

826
00:56:31,610 --> 00:56:42,396
[SPEAKER_00]: But you want to see that to the point of where you have completely, completely, sort of, any desire towards it has faded away, towards sensual enjoyment.

827
00:56:42,997 --> 00:56:47,259
[SPEAKER_00]: And it will fade away when the danger becomes perception of danger, basically.

828
00:56:47,539 --> 00:56:52,062
[SPEAKER_00]: That background implication, significance of danger is developed to the extent necessary.

829
00:56:53,256 --> 00:57:00,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby, no matter how much you get distracted, you'll never ever go back to the point of being distracted that you actually want sensuality.

830
00:57:00,760 --> 00:57:05,122
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, like you vomit... you vomit the food that you ate.

831
00:57:05,382 --> 00:57:11,746
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll never ever gonna get so hungry to eat that stuff that's moldy and filthy and mixed with soil.

832
00:57:11,826 --> 00:57:14,327
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just... it's just not perceived as edible anymore.

833
00:57:14,627 --> 00:57:15,788
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how far you wanna go.

834
00:57:16,228 --> 00:57:20,231
[SPEAKER_00]: But sensuality, it ceased to be perceived as worthy of engagement.

835
00:57:20,391 --> 00:57:21,311
[SPEAKER_00]: It's actually the opposite.

836
00:57:21,972 --> 00:57:23,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-engagement is what's worthy.

837
00:57:23,953 --> 00:57:25,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-engagement is what freedom is.

838
00:57:25,954 --> 00:57:27,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Engagement is death.

839
00:57:28,416 --> 00:57:29,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Results in death.

840
00:57:29,317 --> 00:57:30,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Implies death.

841
00:57:30,978 --> 00:57:33,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Makes you liable to death.

842
00:57:33,759 --> 00:57:35,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally.

843
00:57:35,160 --> 00:57:38,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, like, you wouldn't be killed if somebody comes and shoots you in the head.

844
00:57:39,143 --> 00:57:39,923
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not death.

845
00:57:40,844 --> 00:57:43,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Death is sending your mind back down the senses.

846
00:57:44,286 --> 00:57:45,347
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you get killed.

847
00:57:46,550 --> 00:57:53,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you take your mind away from the senses, you surmount sensuality, even if somebody comes and shoots you in the head, you are not killed.

848
00:57:54,573 --> 00:58:03,376
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why Buddha said those who are not mindful, those who are not properly established upon the peripheral background discernment of that significance, they are as if they're already dead.

849
00:58:11,215 --> 00:58:19,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you see the extent of sensuality, then you know very well when when you step into it.

850
00:58:20,085 --> 00:58:20,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

851
00:58:20,546 --> 00:58:21,607
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be done accidentally.

852
00:58:21,948 --> 00:58:22,688
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always a choice.

853
00:58:23,507 --> 00:58:24,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the thing.

854
00:58:24,247 --> 00:58:24,987
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always a choice.

855
00:58:25,287 --> 00:58:31,689
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you see the extent of sensuality, you also see your choice of... the choice.

856
00:58:32,229 --> 00:58:35,810
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're aware of the background, you're aware of you about to make a choice.

857
00:58:35,830 --> 00:58:40,571
[SPEAKER_00]: You're aware of the possibilities to choose, which means you realize, oh, I'm already responsible.

858
00:58:41,151 --> 00:58:45,592
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm already choosing, basically, a direction of sensuality, even before I directly engage with it.

859
00:58:46,272 --> 00:58:48,193
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's basically seeing the choice of sensuality.

860
00:58:48,233 --> 00:58:48,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

861
00:58:48,453 --> 00:58:49,493
[SPEAKER_00]: It's also seeing the extent of sensuality.

862
00:58:49,513 --> 00:58:50,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing the extent of sensuality.

863
00:58:50,534 --> 00:58:51,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing the background of sensuality, yeah.

864
00:58:52,214 --> 00:58:54,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning gradual significance of it, yeah.

865
00:58:55,295 --> 00:58:56,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking responsibility for a choice.

866
00:58:57,536 --> 00:58:58,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

867
00:58:58,817 --> 00:59:00,237
[SPEAKER_00]: That's another approach to the same thing.

